# Example reverse proxy for a SaaS/single-VPS deployment (see
# ../NETWORK_TOPOLOGY.md). Caddy is the only process that binds a public
# interface — it terminates TLS (automatic, via Let's Encrypt) and proxies
# to four things, on separate domains: TrustPay Backend (the consumer
# app's own traffic), the gateway (external bank/developer API traffic,
# tiered keys, rate limited — services/gateway), Checkout's own narrow
# public surface (a hosted pay page and inbound provider webhooks), and
# Identity's admin console (staff back-office UI plus its own API — see
# both narrow-exception explanations below). Different audiences,
# deliberately kept on different domains rather than one proxy routing
# by path. Ledger, Payments, Compliance, and Cards are not reachable
# through this or any other route from outside the box; they bind
# 127.0.0.1 themselves regardless of what's in front of them.

trustpay.example.com {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8082
}

# Also serves the tenant admin dashboard (services/gateway/admin-console,
# a bank's own technical/integration admin managing API keys, usage,
# sandbox status) at /console — no separate domain needed, unlike
# Identity's own console: the gateway was already this platform's public
# tenant-facing surface before the dashboard existed, so it just coexists
# here alongside /docs and the tenant-proxy routes.
api.trustbank.example.com {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8084
}

# Checkout is loopback-bound like every other service — this is a
# narrow, deliberate exception to "nothing but the two domains above is
# public": a hosted pay page has to be reachable by an anonymous
# customer's browser, and an inbound provider webhook has to be
# reachable by Paystack's (or another provider's) servers, and neither
# holds a tenant API key to go through the gateway's proxy. Only these
# two path prefixes are ever exposed — the merchant/checkout-session
# CRUD API stays exactly as internal as any other service's, reached
# only through the gateway (see ../NETWORK_TOPOLOGY.md's own section on
# this).
pay.trustbank.example.com {
	@public path /pay/* /v1/webhooks/*
	reverse_proxy @public 127.0.0.1:8087
	respond 404
}

# Identity's admin console (services/identity) — staff back-office UI
# plus its full API underneath it. Unlike every other service, Identity's
# entire /v1/* surface is meant to be reached directly from a staff
# member's browser outside the box: every route already requires an
# authenticated, MFA-backed staff session (or a specific RBAC role) —
# the auth model was built for this from the start, not added on top of
# an internal-only assumption. A full site proxy, not path-scoped like
# Checkout's block above — there's no "hide everything except two paths"
# boundary to enforce here, since every path is already authenticated;
# the app itself is the boundary. See ../NETWORK_TOPOLOGY.md's "Identity's
# public posture" section for the full reasoning.
ops.trustbank.example.com {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8085
}

# Nothing else is proxied. Do not add routes for :8080 (Ledger), :8081
# (Payments), :8083 (Compliance), or any other path on :8087 (Checkout)
# beyond pay.trustbank.example.com's own scoped block above — if a
# future admin/ops tool for one of *those* services needs to reach it
# from outside the box, put it behind its own authenticated route (the
# way Identity's own console now is) or a VPN, not a bare reverse_proxy.
